# Two arms whose first positions can never hold the same value.
#
# Should: compile, since no value is both `none` and a string, and print
# "nothing loud a other 3". Chapter 03 says arms are rejected when "dispatch
# could never tell them apart"; these can always be told apart by the first
# argument alone.
#
# Does: `kanso play disjoint_arms_called_a_tie.kso` refuses with
#   error[dispatch]: these `show` arms tie: each is the more specific one
#   somewhere, and a call could match both
# pointing at the string arm. The ranking compares position by position
# (none beats string in the first, true beats _ in the second) without
# asking whether the two first positions overlap at all.
fn show none _
  "nothing"

fn show s:string true
  "loud {s}"

fn show v _
  "other {v}"

print "{show none true} {show "a" true} {show 3 false}"
